    "['unit [unit_number] family [family_type]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/0', 'parent_command': ['interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1'], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/0\\n mpls ip\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/1\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco commands enable MPLS on the specified interfaces, which is equivalent to the Juniper \"family mpls\" configuration under the interface units. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of logical units (unit 0) or separate configuration for interface families in the same way Juniper does.', 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/0\\n mpls ip\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/1\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco commands enable MPLS on the specified interfaces, which is equivalent to the Juniper \"family mpls\" configuration under the interface units. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of logical units (unit 0) or separate configuration for interface families in the same way Juniper does.', 'trans_template': 'mpls ip'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/1', 'parent_command': ['interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1'], 'root': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/0\\n mpls ip\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/1\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco commands enable MPLS on the specified interfaces, which is equivalent to the Juniper \"family mpls\" configuration under the interface units. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of logical units (unit 0) or separate configuration for interface families in the same way Juniper does.', 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet0/1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/0\\n mpls ip\\ninterface GigabitEthernet0/1\\n mpls ip\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco commands enable MPLS on the specified interfaces, which is equivalent to the Juniper \"family mpls\" configuration under the interface units. Note that Cisco doesn\\'t use the concept of logical units (unit 0) or separate configuration for interface families in the same way Juniper does.', 'trans_template': 'mpls ip'}]]": 1,
