    "['local-address [localIP]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp [AS_NUMBER]', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP local-address configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp [AS_NUMBER]\\nneighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source 11.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you would need to:\\n1. First specify the router's BGP AS number (replace [AS_NUMBER] with your actual AS)\\n2. The local address is configured under each neighbor using the update-source parameter\\n3. The command assumes the neighbor 11.1.1.2 is already configured (as shown in the original Juniper config)\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp [AS_NUMBER]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source 11.1.1.1', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP local-address configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp [AS_NUMBER]\\nneighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source 11.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you would need to:\\n1. First specify the router's BGP AS number (replace [AS_NUMBER] with your actual AS)\\n2. The local address is configured under each neighbor using the update-source parameter\\n3. The command assumes the neighbor 11.1.1.2 is already configured (as shown in the original Juniper config)\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source 11.1.1.1'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [neighbor_ip] peer-as [peer_as_number]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you first need to enter the BGP process with the local AS number (100 in this case, as indicated by the peer-as 100 in the original command), then configure the neighbor with its AS number. The Juniper command implicitly uses the local-address 11.1.1.1 which would be configured separately in Cisco as \"neighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source\" if needed.', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local'], 'root': 'aaa authentication login [parameter1] group [parameter2] local', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you first need to enter the BGP process with the local AS number (100 in this case, as indicated by the peer-as 100 in the original command), then configure the neighbor with its AS number. The Juniper command implicitly uses the local-address 11.1.1.1 which would be configured separately in Cisco as \"neighbor 11.1.1.2 update-source\" if needed.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 11.1.1.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
