    "['set policy-options policy-statement [policy_name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco IOS configuration for the given Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n match protocol bgp\\n set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nThis translation:\\n1. Converts Juniper's `policy-statement` to Cisco's `route-map`\\n2. The `term 1` becomes sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses increments of 10)\\n3. `from protocol bgp` becomes `match protocol bgp`\\n4. `next-hop self` remains conceptually the same in Cisco\\n5. `accept` is implied by the `permit` action in the route-map statement\", 'trans_template': 'route-map policy1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol bgp', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'match protocol bgp', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco IOS configuration for the given Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n match protocol bgp\\n set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nThis translation:\\n1. Converts Juniper's `policy-statement` to Cisco's `route-map`\\n2. The `term 1` becomes sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses increments of 10)\\n3. `from protocol bgp` becomes `match protocol bgp`\\n4. `next-hop self` remains conceptually the same in Cisco\\n5. `accept` is implied by the `permit` action in the route-map statement\", 'trans_template': 'match protocol bgp'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ip next-hop self', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set ip next-hop self', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco IOS configuration for the given Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n match protocol bgp\\n set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nThis translation:\\n1. Converts Juniper's `policy-statement` to Cisco's `route-map`\\n2. The `term 1` becomes sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses increments of 10)\\n3. `from protocol bgp` becomes `match protocol bgp`\\n4. `next-hop self` remains conceptually the same in Cisco\\n5. `accept` is implied by the `permit` action in the route-map statement\", 'trans_template': 'set ip next-hop self'}]]": 1,
    "['term [term_number] from protocol [protocol_name]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\nmatch protocol bgp\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco IOS version uses:\\n1. `route-map` instead of `policy-statement`\\n2. `permit` action (equivalent to Juniper's `accept`)\\n3. Sequence number `10` (equivalent to Juniper's term `1`)\\n4. `match protocol` instead of `from protocol`\", 'trans_template': 'route-map policy1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol bgp', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\nmatch protocol bgp\\n##\\n\\nThe Cisco IOS version uses:\\n1. `route-map` instead of `policy-statement`\\n2. `permit` action (equivalent to Juniper's `accept`)\\n3. Sequence number `10` (equivalent to Juniper's term `1`)\\n4. `match protocol` instead of `from protocol`\", 'trans_template': 'match protocol bgp'}]]": 1,
    "['term [term_number] then next-hop self', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, `route-map` is used instead of Juniper's `policy-statement`\\n- `permit 10` corresponds to `term 1` (with sequence number 10 being equivalent to term 1)\\n- `set ip next-hop self` is the direct equivalent of `then next-hop self`\", 'trans_template': 'route-map policy1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ip next-hop self', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper policy statement:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Cisco, `route-map` is used instead of Juniper's `policy-statement`\\n- `permit 10` corresponds to `term 1` (with sequence number 10 being equivalent to term 1)\\n- `set ip next-hop self` is the direct equivalent of `then next-hop self`\", 'trans_template': 'set ip next-hop self'}]]": 1,
    "['term [term_number] then [action]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent for the Juniper command:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n  match ip address prefix-list (create corresponding prefix-list if needed)\\n  set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n  \\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"route-map\" replaces Juniper\\'s \"policy-statement\"\\n2. \"permit\" is equivalent to \"accept\"\\n3. The term number (10 in this case) replaces Juniper\\'s term numbering\\n4. The \"set ip next-hop self\" is maintained from the previous term action\\n5. Cisco requires an explicit match statement (not present in the original Juniper config), so you would need to create a matching prefix-list that corresponds to your BGP routes', 'trans_template': 'route-map policy1 permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match ip address prefix-list (create corresponding prefix-list if needed)', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent for the Juniper command:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n  match ip address prefix-list (create corresponding prefix-list if needed)\\n  set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n  \\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"route-map\" replaces Juniper\\'s \"policy-statement\"\\n2. \"permit\" is equivalent to \"accept\"\\n3. The term number (10 in this case) replaces Juniper\\'s term numbering\\n4. The \"set ip next-hop self\" is maintained from the previous term action\\n5. Cisco requires an explicit match statement (not present in the original Juniper config), so you would need to create a matching prefix-list that corresponds to your BGP routes', 'trans_template': 'match ip address prefix-list (create corresponding prefix-list if needed)'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ip next-hop self', 'parent_command': ['route-map policy1 permit 10'], 'root': 'route-map policy1 permit 10', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent for the Juniper command:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map policy1 permit 10\\n  match ip address prefix-list (create corresponding prefix-list if needed)\\n  set ip next-hop self\\n##\\n  \\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"route-map\" replaces Juniper\\'s \"policy-statement\"\\n2. \"permit\" is equivalent to \"accept\"\\n3. The term number (10 in this case) replaces Juniper\\'s term numbering\\n4. The \"set ip next-hop self\" is maintained from the previous term action\\n5. Cisco requires an explicit match statement (not present in the original Juniper config), so you would need to create a matching prefix-list that corresponds to your BGP routes', 'trans_template': 'set ip next-hop self'}]]": 1,
